sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch04-12> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Most if not all of the intracellular phenomena of mitosis follow inevitably from very simple assumptions about the physical constitution of the cell and a simple distribution of polarised forces within it, and are likely to prove of only remote and secondary biological significance.";
    dc:type "conceptual";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Mitotic phenomena follow from simple physical assumptions and have secondary significance";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter IV, “On the Internal Form and Structure of the Cell”; topic “Biological significance of mitotic figures”. Scope: intracellular phenomena of cell division in plants and animals"
}
